// Rejected at resolve (typing): `promote` followed by `demote` does not make a
// tie-prone key tie-free.  The scan is still undetermined
// (`Mensura.IsArrangement.unique`, ADR 0029 Decision 11).
//
// The reason is the inverse-pair property itself (ADR 0024): a key move derives
// cardinality from the gradings and never invents one, so `demote (promote T)`
// is a no-op on the facts as well as on the content.  It would be unsound for
// it to be otherwise, since moving a tie-prone column through the key cannot
// make two rows that share a timestamp stop sharing it.
//
// What licenses the scan is a *reduction* between the two moves, whose
// `singletons` output records the finer key as a grading; that is the sibling
// `accept/aggregation_establishes_a_tie_free_key.mensura`.  Deleting the
// reduction from that file gives this one.  The other fix is
// `assume { arranged }`, tier 3, as in
// `accept/scan_with_a_descending_key.mensura`.
import series

unit Machine {
  id: string
}

store readings {
  unit { Machine }
  attr* {
    taken_at: date
    temperature: real
  }
}

view bad {
  readings
    |> promote taken_at
    |> demote taken_at
    |> map_bags |k, b| (
      .running_peak = series.running_max (|r| r.temperature) (|r| r.taken_at) b
    )
}
